{"name":"Acorn Squash","hex":"#eda740","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acorn squash","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several varieties of a plant, Cucurbita pepo, being a dark green winter squash having yellow to orange flesh."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acorn_squash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acorn squash","blocks":[{"text":"Acorn squash, also called pepper squash or Des Moines squash, is a winter squash with distinctive longitudinal ridges on its exterior and sweet, yellow-orange flesh inside. Although considered a winter squash, acorn squash belongs to the same species as all summer squashes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Starr_070730-7820_Cucurbita_pepo.jpg/330px-Starr_070730-7820_Cucurbita_pepo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorn_squash","wikidata":"Q2975815"},{"title":"Acorn","blocks":[{"text":"The acorn is the nut of the oaks and their close relatives ; alternatively known as glans. It usually contains a seedling surrounded by two cotyledons, enclosed in a tough shell known as the pericarp, and borne in a cup-shaped cupule. Acorns take between 5 and 24 months to mature; see the list of Quercus species for details of oak classification, in which acorn morphology and phenology are important factors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorn","wikidata":"Q3914781"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Starr_070730-7820_Cucurbita_pepo.jpg/500px-Starr_070730-7820_Cucurbita_pepo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Starr_070730-7820_Cucurbita